# Moybyte T-Deck board fragment (mainline build).
#
# Everything here is either a BOARD FACT (flash size/mode, partition table) or a
# lever that was A/B'd on THIS board's glass and shipped in the fork build. The
# fork's knobs that are NOT here are deliberate; see the README's "what the fork
# tunes that this build does not (yet)" table.

# 16MB flash. The generic S3 board assumes 4MB.
CONFIG_ESPTOOLPY_FLASHSIZE_16MB=y

# DIO, not the generic board's QIO: the images this repo actually flashes to a
# T-Deck are the dio ones (Makefile MPY_FLASH_MODE ?= dio), and the mode in the
# image header is what the ROM bootloader uses.
CONFIG_ESPTOOLPY_FLASHMODE_QIO=
CONFIG_ESPTOOLPY_FLASHMODE_DIO=y
CONFIG_ESPTOOLPY_FLASHFREQ_80M=y

# The dual-OTA partition table (#53). IDENTICAL to what the fork build's
# --ota --flash-size=16 --partition-size=5242880 generates, offset for offset:
# both boards in the field are on this table and a change costs every deployed
# device a full-erase USB flash. CONFIG_PARTITION_TABLE_CUSTOM_FILENAME
# resolves relative to ports/esp32, so build.sh stages the CSV there.
CONFIG_PARTITION_TABLE_CUSTOM=y
CONFIG_PARTITION_TABLE_CUSTOM_FILENAME="partitions-moybyte-tdeck.csv"

# Rollback (#53): an app that never calls
# esp32.Partition.mark_app_valid_cancel_rollback() is reverted by the
# bootloader on the next boot, so a bad OTA self-heals. sdkconfig.base already
# sets this upstream; restated here because build.sh's stale-sdkconfig guard
# greps the GENERATED config for it -- an existing build dir silently keeps
# whatever it was first configured with.
CONFIG_BOOTLOADER_APP_ROLLBACK_ENABLE=y

# Cache geometry (#63, A/B'd on this board): the stock build runs the S3's
# MINIMUM caches (16KB icache / 32KB dcache) while the VM reads frozen bytecode
# from flash AND the gc heap from PSRAM through that one small dcache, with the
# panel DMA streaming through the same path. Doubling both is worth ~2.5x on kid
# float loops. Costs 48KB of internal SRAM.
#
# The LINE stays 32B. A first pass also widened lines to 64B and the hardware
# answered with horizontal garbage bands on every screen -- the 64B coherency
# granularity breaks the CPU<->GDMA handoff in the PSRAM flush path. Do not
# re-widen without an on-hardware A/B.
CONFIG_ESP32S3_INSTRUCTION_CACHE_16KB=
CONFIG_ESP32S3_INSTRUCTION_CACHE_32KB=y
CONFIG_ESP32S3_DATA_CACHE_32KB=
CONFIG_ESP32S3_DATA_CACHE_64KB=y
CONFIG_ESP32S3_DATA_CACHE_LINE_32B=y

# PSRAM at 120MHz, matching the fork. THIS IS THE PORT'S LAST MEASURED GAP and
# it was not a code difference at all: the mainline board set no SPIRAM speed, so
# it took the 80MHz default while the shipping fork has run 120M OCT for months.
#
# Measured on the owner's board, same cart, same night, after the flush overlap
# had already landed:
#
#     render= minus bg=   fork 7.73ms   mainline 7.93ms   (matches to 0.2ms)
#     bg= (a 153,600 B PSRAM fill)   4.06ms   vs   5.99ms
#     implied write rate  37.8 MB/s   vs   25.6 MB/s   = 1.475x
#     clock ratio         120 / 80                     = 1.5x
#
# Everything else was already equal -- same cache geometry (checked in the
# generated sdkconfig, not assumed), same staged kernel at the same -O3, 240MHz
# on both. The ~2ms lives in the MSPI clock and nowhere else, and the `logic=`
# tax (1.50 -> 2.00) is the same clock on the PSRAM-resident gc heap.
#
# 120M is ESP-IDF "experimental" and temperature-sensitive, so this is a risk
# decision rather than free -- but it is the SAME risk the shipping fork has
# carried on this exact hardware, so matching it is the conservative choice for a
# replacement build, not a new gamble. If the panel ever shows garbage bands or
# the board wedges under sustained load, this is the first line to revert; note
# the cache LINE above stays 32B for a related and separately-learned reason.
# 120M is not one flag. It is Kconfig-gated behind IDF_EXPERIMENTAL_FEATURES,
# it requires the FLASH at 120M too, and the S3's 120M PSRAM timing DRIFTS WITH
# TEMPERATURE -- so the fork also enables the temperature-sensor retune on a 5s
# interval. Setting SPIRAM_SPEED_120M alone silently leaves you at 80M, which is
# exactly what a first attempt here did (the generated sdkconfig still read
# SPEED=80 after a clean build). All five together, matching the fork's `fast`
# profile, or none of them.
# External MSPI speed: octal flash + octal PSRAM at 120MHz. This is the SAME
# flag set the shipping fork applies (its build.sh MOYBYTE_EXTMEM_SPEED=fast) --
# keep the two together rather than deriving this one from Kconfig again.
#
# Flash and PSRAM move TOGETHER: at 240MHz core, ESP-IDF 5.5.1 ships no MSPI
# timing table for PSRAM-120 with flash-80, and says so as a compile error
# naming the 240M/120M table. Do not set a FLASHMODE here -- the fork sets none,
# the octal variant decides it.
#
# THIS REQUIRES esp_psram_temp_retune_any_vendor.patch (#169), applied by
# build.sh. IDF only starts the temperature retune for verified flash vendor IDs
# (0xC8/0x20) and otherwise returns ESP_ERR_NOT_SUPPORTED from a SECONDARY
# ESP_SYSTEM_INIT_FN -- which ABORTS THE BOOT. Enabling the retune without the
# patch gives a board that flashes cleanly, says nothing on serial and never
# reaches the console; that was measured here on 2026-08-16 and read for an hour
# as a PSRAM timing problem, which it is not.
#
# Do NOT read the generated sdkconfig's STRINGS to check this landed:
#   CONFIG_ESPTOOLPY_FLASHFREQ_120M=y   <- the choice, what matters
#   CONFIG_ESPTOOLPY_FLASHFREQ="80m"    <- correct, and stays 80m
# The flash image header can only encode 80/40/26/20MHz and must stay ROM
# readable, so 120M is applied at runtime by the MSPI tuning code. Reading the
# string is what made this look like a setting that would not apply, twice.
CONFIG_IDF_EXPERIMENTAL_FEATURES=y
CONFIG_ESPTOOLPY_FLASHFREQ_80M=
CONFIG_ESPTOOLPY_FLASHFREQ_120M=y
CONFIG_SPIRAM_SPEED_80M=
CONFIG_SPIRAM_SPEED_120M=y
CONFIG_SPIRAM_TIMING_TUNING_POINT_VIA_TEMPERATURE_SENSOR=y
CONFIG_SPIRAM_TIMING_MEASURE_TEMPERATURE_INTERVAL_SECOND=5

# USB-Serial-JTAG as the PRIMARY console, so serial INPUT works (#201).
#
# The board previously left the IDF default: UART0 primary, USB-Serial-JTAG
# SECONDARY. A secondary console is OUTPUT ONLY -- Espressif's own Kconfig help
# says so: "This secondary output currently only supports non-blocking mode
# without using REPL. If you want to output in blocking mode with REPL or input
# through this secondary port, please change the primary config to this port."
#
# That is the whole reason typed input never arrived on this board: TX flowed
# over USB (secondary, output-only) while INPUT was taken from the primary,
# UART0 -- whose RX is GPIO44 on the expansion header, unconnected and floating.
# Hence a single stray byte in 150 seconds (`SERIAL rx=1 lines=0 partial=1`) and
# a host write() that timed out having transferred ZERO bytes: nothing on the
# device was ever reading that endpoint.
#
# It is NOT micropython#18581 (CDC-init-at-REPL), which is what this was first
# diagnosed as -- TinyUSB is not even linked into this image (`tusb_init` and
# `tud_cdc_rx_cb` are both absent from the elf). No TinyUSB is needed: the S3's
# USB-Serial-JTAG peripheral is bidirectional on its own once it is primary.
#
# The same misconfiguration is why the SHIPPING FORK has no serial harness
# either, and why CLAUDE.md carried a wrong explanation for it for months.
#
# UART REPL stays on (mpconfigboard.h) -- it costs nothing and keeps the header
# pins usable with a USB-UART adapter, which is how the P4 has always worked
# (its CH343 is exactly that, soldered on).
# REVERTED 2026-08-16 AFTER FLASHING IT: making USB-Serial-JTAG the PRIMARY
# console fixed RX at the USB level -- a host write() went from timing out at 0
# bytes to being ACCEPTED -- and then the board emitted NOTHING and stopped
# booting. The primary console is BLOCKING (the same Kconfig help that says the
# secondary cannot take input says the primary is the one that supports
# "blocking mode with REPL"), so the desktop hangs on its first print when no
# host is draining. Recovery needed esptool --before default_reset, which does
# work on this board even with the app wedged.
#
# RESOLVED 2026-08-16, and the two halves explain each other. That hang happened
# while MICROPY_HW_ESP_USB_SERIAL_JTAG was still 0 (USBDEV was on), so
# MicroPython had NO tx path of its own and mp_hal_stdout_tx_strn fell through to
# IDF's primary console -- which blocks. With USBDEV off (see mpconfigboard.h)
# MicroPython uses its own usb_serial_jtag_tx_strn instead: an absent host costs
# one 200ms timeout, then terminal_connected latches false and every later write
# returns immediately. Non-blocking by construction.
#
# So neither change works alone -- promoting the console alone hangs the board,
# linking the ISR alone leaves the console secondary and input-only-in-theory --
# and that is why each was measured as a failure on its own. They ship together.
CONFIG_ESP_CONSOLE_UART_DEFAULT=
CONFIG_ESP_CONSOLE_SECONDARY_USB_SERIAL_JTAG=
CONFIG_ESP_CONSOLE_USB_SERIAL_JTAG=y
CONFIG_ESP_CONSOLE_SECONDARY_NONE=y
